      },
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Verb–noun order", "html": "The person comes straight after the form of <b>bí</b>, then <b>ag</b>, then the verbal noun. The verbal noun never changes for person: <i>Tá mé ag rith</i>, <i>Tá siad ag rith</i>." },
        { "title": "ag before a vowel", "html": "In careful writing <b>ag</b> is kept before a vowel (<i>ag ól</i>, <i>ag ithe</i>, <i>ag éisteacht</i>), though you will hear it shortened to <i>a</i> in speech." },
        { "title": "Negative & questions", "html": "Use the <b>bí</b> forms <i>Níl</i>, <i>An bhfuil?</i> and <i>Nach bhfuil?</i> — the <i>ag</i> + verbal noun part stays the same: <i>Níl mé ag léamh</i>." }
      ]
